      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "value": "Just another thought, after our Endgame trolls episode: We had someone in there who was kind of trolling, and when we confronted him about it he was very upset. He thought he was just being funny. But in the dynamic exchange between the \"hosts\" of the room/show (us) and him, he learned what behavior was appropriate and changed to adhere to our rules/expectations. Had he just been kicked with a generic warning, or even warned anonymously, the social implication of these anonymous \"judgers\" might be a very negative/anger-eliciting experience. Trolls are sensitive underneath, and can very easily be motivated down dark paths of revenge/anger (they also happen to be high in psychopathy, according to a recent study: http://www.sciencedirect.com.proxy.library.vanderbilt.edu/science/article/pii/S0191886917304270?_rdoc=1&_fmt=high&_origin=gateway&_docanchor=&md5=b8429449ccfc9c30159a5f9aeaa92ffb&ccp=y )",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
