    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "5adfce27d198de6f75b8db6c",
      "author": {
        "_id": "5aba63bd5c8cf50a0500fb8e",
        "alias": "Yarrow Angelfish",
        "aliasID": "65c2c188ed6eefce4c8dc99c",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny.io/images/ffcfc301e1f797352a2b0ae76a95d8ca.png",
        "created": "2018-03-27T15:31:09.431Z",
        "name": "Takatsuki",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2018-04-25T00:39:03.643Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5a3ad7fd682bb9742d24a0f2",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "value": "I am a little late to this, but I just noticed it whilst adding some requests.\n\nI think it's extremely important, given that that the game is non-profit, and has a small team working on it. Bringing Runescape up, I believe that their system actually works fairly well. I have a friend whom actually works on their anti-cheat team and the active player moderators do a very good job keeping the peace when necessary. \n\nI also believe that player moderators would need a greater level of power in VRChat. This is mainly to deal with those that are abusing the leniency of SDK restriction, anti-cheat etc. Having administrated a Rust server for close to three years now, I understand just how badly one player can affect a playerbase if they aren't dealt with responsibly and correctly.\n\nI'd go as far as saying that although it changes how you play the game, most players would happily take up the responsibility, even if it meant hopping into a world to deal with a \"mod request\" for 10 minutes. It'd be satisfying know that it's one less person to bump into or ruin the game for others if it's something requiring a temporary/permanent suspension from the game.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "5b39134cba28ae3550c106b2",
      "author": {
        "_id": "5b319f9e3a74122b586f5e84",
        "alias": "Violet Sturgeon",
        "aliasID": "65c5c3bced6eefce4c1f5970",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": null,
        "created": "2018-06-26T02:06:22.011Z",
        "name": "Emnolope",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2018-07-01T17:45:48.256Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5a3ad7fd682bb9742d24a0f2",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I believe that VRChat should remain like the wild west for as long as possible. I am a hacker, and some people want just to see what's possible, and others just want to make people miserable. We should aim to create an environment where engaging in unpleasant acts, entail an unpleasant response, without limiting the freedom and information privacy of creative types like me. I would hate to see this game become overly restrictive, but I also don't like the hushing effect that's causing people to start making private worlds.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "5b391503ba28ae3550c106f1",
      "author": {
        "_id": "5b319f9e3a74122b586f5e84",
        "alias": "Violet Sturgeon",
        "aliasID": "65c5c3bced6eefce4c1f5970",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": null,
        "created": "2018-06-26T02:06:22.011Z",
        "name": "Emnolope",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2018-07-01T17:53:07.039Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5a3ad7fd682bb9742d24a0f2",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "In all, aberrant, anti-social, and harmful behavior are 5% in most groups, but their effects are seen by everyone. We should make an infrastructure that limits how they can even harm others in the first place. If someone takes my pen because it's too sharp, I can take a pencil and sharpen it to a shank, if they shorten the pencil, I can take many of them and turn them into little claws. Any tool of any kind can be used to harm others, and trying to take those away won't help, instead we need to make an environment where harm intrinsically is unpleasant. If I'm going to be judged by peers, it doesn't matter if I use a shank, pencil, or even a spoon to harm somebody, I'm going to be judged and there will be consequences of that, which would prevent me from using ANY MEANS of harm to another individual",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
