      "value": "Community mods has been tossed around a few times, but for now, it isn't a viable solution. Since there has been very little activity on this lately, I'll go ahead and close it.\n\nWe are working on ways to improve the ability of users to self-moderate and keep themselves safe while in VRChat, while also improving the effectiveness of our Moderation team.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2017-12-20T21:37:01.542Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Hello all! It has been exciting to watch VRChat grow since the days of the Dk2. I’ve had interesting discussions thus far with the VRchat team and community members regarding the possibilities for moderation. I’ve also observed how public rooms have changed as an influx of streamers and new players has increased.\n\nAs some of you know, I am getting my PhD in clinical psychology and am learning strategies on how to help people change their behavior. After evaluating behavioral trends of new players in VRChat, I believe it is necessary for an environmental change to elicit a pro-social set of behaviors and foster positive community growth.\n\nI’m going to try to focus on the domains that I believe are most important. I have gone back through to read the previous canny.io mod suggestions to ensure I do not repeat things, and so that I have a better understanding of what has already been suggested. So, to begin I will summarize excerpts of a recent Canny reply post made by Ron:\n\n------\n“-Deputizing community members globally is not ideal. At least not in the short term. That would likely remain something we do as a team\n-Systems should exist to enable world authors to self moderate alone or with those they choose to deputize. Another set of systems that will help is a more granular social graph including groups, favorites and stats. \n-Tracked user behavior (stats) should also help define how trusted a user is globally. The systems of which would not be fully disclosed much like the real world or online systems such as that used for twitter verification. For example: If someone has 1 friend and has been blocked 20 times in a day and warned by a mod chances are they are trouble vs someone who's made 20 friends, uploaded a world with lots of visits and that we've met and talked to in VRChat.”\n-----\n\nJust a note: in my suggestions, by “deputized” I mean mods that are chosen based off some criteria (some of which could be automatic, as mentioned by Ron earlier...e.g., account age). Also, when I refer to “mods” I am referring to non-employee community members; Tupper had a great list of games where this is done (in reference to concerns about mods being employees of VRchat).\n\n(1) Deputized mods can facilitate faster prosocial learning: As new players join VRchat, if they see another player with some kind of “mod” tag, they are presented with the reality that VRchat has a set of rules. Over time players will also get to know the VRchat “commandments.” Mods would politely ask players to change behavior if it’s violating community rules (e.g., “Hey man! Could you cut down on the profanity?”) Compare two new player experiences: \n\n(A) Player enters the hub and hears someone yelling racial slurs. Nothing happens; player makes assumptions about the entire community of VRchat \n(B) Player enters hub and hears someone yelling racial slurs. A mod walks over to ask the person to stop; when they don’t, they receive a 24 hr public ban\nIn experience (A) the player learns that it is okay to use profanity. In experience (B), the player is exposed to community rules and learns that when a mod is present, these rules must be abided by.\n\nJust to note - it’s important that these deputized mods are visible to other players. Without this, players may be kicked without being warned beforehand, and other players will not be “influenced” into pro-social norms in the same way. Horrible trolls are created when a player feels they are unfairly banned...if there are some warnings/general social pressure it can help a lot. \n\n\n(2) The whole “I want to be a mod” thing can change an entire community: Let’s face it...whenever it’s possible for players to become mods, some people focus on engaging in behaviors to increase those chances. I saw this in Runescape...but it can actually help. Once people seek to become mods, the “guidance” from the VRChat admin team becomes very powerful. People will jump through whatever hoops you create, and you can elicit behavioral control over shaping the VRchat community in the way you want.\n\n(3) Public space in VRChat are inherently different from private ones. The VRChat team had a great update with the “public ban” that emphasizes this difference. It seems the “self-moderation” approach is encouraged at the moment. This may work for invite- or friends-only rooms, but this does not address the “core” of VRChat (public rooms with new users). In these spaces, anyone can join -- we need visual cues of “rule enforcers” who will enforce a fair set of community standards. This will drastically change the experience of public players. \n\n(4) VRchat is scaling beyond the admin team’s moderation abilities. My real fear of what could happen is the main motivation for writing this post: VRchat is changing and has an organic community. If we do not act quickly enough to shape it the way we hope, it could turn ugly very quickly. The general model of having a VRChat admin in a room for events and such has worked great. \n\nLastly, one question that has been contemplated: what if the average troll makes up 50% of the potential VRChat user base? Can these moderation tools/approaches decrease VRchat growth? I propose an answer: if you create the opportunity for private spaces away from “mods” then these “troll” users will have their own places to grow. If they continue to exist in the public spaces, however, you will lose many users who come into VRchat and have negative initial experiences. \n\nAnyway, those are some of my thoughts about the state of VRchat and our need for public, visible moderators. I’m curious what other people think - thank you for taking the time to read my post!",
